---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../data/catalog';
import { breadcrumbSchema, localBusinessSchema, organizationSchema } from '../schema';

const origin = Astro.site?.href ?? Astro.url.origin;

const schema = [
  localBusinessSchema(origin),
  breadcrumbSchema(origin, [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Trade counter' }]),
  organizationSchema(origin),
];

const hours = [
  { days: 'Monday to Friday', open: '07:30', close: '17:00' },
  { days: 'Saturday', open: '08:00', close: '12:00' },
  { days: 'Sunday', open: 'Closed', close: '' },
];
---

<!--
	The title names the business, because the schema does. Calling the page
	"Trade counter" while the Store node is "Acme Tools Trade Counter" is the
	disagreement the share preview reports — and it reported this one, which is
	how it came to be fixed.
-->
<Layout
	title={`${SITE_NAME} Trade Counter`}
	lede="A physical place, which is a different entity from the company that runs it."
	seo={{
		description:
			'Collect online orders at the Sheffield trade counter. Open 07:30 to 17:00 on weekdays and Saturday mornings.',
		image: '/og/card.png',
		imageAlt: 'Abstract share card for the astro-ai example',
		schema,
	}}
>
	<Callout title="What to try" tone="accent">
		The <strong>Schema</strong> tab draws this as a business, with the address
		flattened out of its <code>PostalAddress</code> and the phone number beside
		it. <code>Store</code> inherits <code>LocalBusiness</code>, so it is checked
		against the same rules without being listed as an unknown type.
	</Callout>

	<section class="details" aria-label="Trade counter details">
		<div>
			<h2>Address</h2>
			<address>1 Forge Lane<br />Sheffield SY S1 2AB</address>
			<p class="phone">+1 555 0100</p>
		</div>
		<div>
			<h2>Opening hours</h2>
			<dl class="hours">
				{
					hours.map(({ days, open, close }) => (
						<div class="row">
							<dt>{days}</dt>
							<dd>{close === '' ? open : `${open} – ${close}`}</dd>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Callout title="Two entities, one page">
		The company and the counter are not the same thing. The
		<code>Organization</code> has a logo and profile links; the
		<code>Store</code> has an address, hours and coordinates. Collapsing them
		into one node is the usual mistake, and it costs the map placement.
	</Callout>
</Layout>

<style>
	.details {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1.25rem;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
	}

	h2 {
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
	}

	address {
		font-style: normal;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}

	.phone {
		color: #86efac;
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
		margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
	}

	.hours {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.3rem;
		margin: 0;
	}

	.row {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		justify-content: space-between;
	}

	.row dt {
		color: #aeb6c5;
	}

	.row dd {
		margin: 0;
	}

	code {
		color: #c4b5fd;
	}
</style>
